Add a Firefox theme generator that follows the QtCurve settings

QtCurve painted the Firefox chrome in the XUL era, and that sheet is
dead. The chrome now takes a colour layer through its custom properties,
and a second writer follows the settings into a profile.

The new binary takes the palette, the owned file, the lock, the watch
and the re-exec code of the GTK writer. It holds a name, a pair of lock
names and an import line of its own. One table in ffmap.cpp binds each
palette source to the properties of the sheet, so one row moves both
arms of the theme. The sheet carries the colour layer alone, because the
next redesign of Firefox moves the widget selectors. The tool box takes
one image for each window state, because the decoration fills the title
bar from the colour of that state.

A new settings key gates every write, and the default is false. A pass
without the key takes the owned sheet away and keeps every foreign line.
The watch mode of a false key removes the files and leaves. The settings
file is inside the watched set, and the save of the dialog starts the
mode again. An opted out user therefore carries no resident process.

The watch set holds the settings, the colour file of KDE, the profile
list of each root and the shared sheet of each profile. Firefox holds no
daemon like kde-gtk-config, which rewrites a file that the GTK watch
reads, so this watch reads the colour file itself.

The import line stands at the front of the shared sheet, because a
parser drops an import that follows a rule. The writer makes the chrome
directory of a profile and never a profile. It reads prefs.js and never
writes it, so a notice names the preference that Firefox needs.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 4 of 8. The branch holds those eight commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve takes work/firefox-theme, and a new merge request on
system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

